            <ArticleTitle>[Recruitment of Families at Risk for Obesity: The Kindergarten Experience].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S25-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1055/s-0033-1333712</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obese children mostly become obese adults. The aim of this study was to test whether the kindergarten setting is a successful access point for universal and selective prevention measures for preschool children and their parents. In a randomised controlled trial, the universal effects on risk and protective factors were observed. But recruitment for the selective programme did not work out which may be due to a biased risk perception of childhood overweight development and high prevention self-efficacy. Alternative strategies are explored.</AbstractText>
